Interpretation:
  1. Strong atom growth is an exact ear insertion problem, not a raw minor move.
  2. The boundary state (starts, ends, Q) is the atom analogue of the LRC
     character-sum ledger: linear boundary terms plus a cut/resonance matrix.
  3. Nonconstant ears preserve strongness automatically when the parent is strong.
  4. Through n=8, strong spectra are fully generated by ears from smaller
     strong atoms; small parent/cut bases cover all observed values.
  5. The forbidden {7,21} values are not minor-closed shadows; they are
     absent solutions of these recursive cut polynomials before the
     strong-min boundary lets the spectrum re-enter densely.

